6|0@hardware^1440685717#cuhgwkm:rdfox$:Remember this is a lawsuit brought by a big company against a former employee. Upon termination, TSMC paid Liang a bunch of money and he agreed not to work for a few years. But after cashing their check, Liang went right back to work. TSMC sued him to enforce the contract and won. How we get from these facts to "Samsung stole TSMC secrets" is just sensationalism. If there were any evidence of that, TSMC would find it far more lucrative to take Samsung to court instead of some guy. Really, TSMC just wants the rest of their engineers to think twice before defecting with whatever general knowledge they may have acquired, as is anyone's right to do.
12|0@hardware^1440694236#cuhmj0l:StreamingPanda$:That's not correct at all. Liang was not terminated, he was made the head of a division of which he deemed was not of value and then resigned, citing that TSMC punished him by not giving him a position he desired. Ironically, the position Liang fled became even more valuable than the position he originally wanted. In his TSMC contract, Liang agreed not to work for 2 years. However, before that period was up, he was employed by Samsung as a Professor at their university (effectively breaching his contract) while telling TSMC he was not. TSMC decided to have faith in him and waited until the full 2 years were up to pay him for not breaching their contract. Within 2 months of this, Liang was made CTO of Samsung's system LSI division. TSMC is completely in the right here.
-3|0@hardware^1440695270#cuhn8mz:rdfox$:I use termination as a non-loaded term for separation of employment. Whether TSMC is in the right is another question. My main point is that Samsung is not in the wrong. TSMC enforced a contract and that may sound like it's okay. The interesting thing is that non-compete clauses for employees are almost never enforced because: * In many places they're made illegal because they're thought to be anti-worker, bad for the economy, and giving employers too much power * Where they are legal, companies don't pursue cases because it's bad for business -- who wants to work for a company that screws its employees for any reason? * Even if brought to court, companies rarely win, absent a smoking gun * At most, the penalty would be to return the severance pay So, I'll allow that TSMC is technically in the right as decided by a court of law. The most troubling thing is that the court did something really strange, ordering an extension to Liang's non-compete clause; worse, the state has a large ownership stake in TSMC, giving this an air of authoritarianism which is not the image Taiwan is trying to cultivate.
1|0@hardware^1440788663#cuixw3f:epsys$:&gt; an extension to Liang's non-compete clause although legally strange, if he breached it, this keeps with the spirit of the law better than simple fines, which are both only nebulously connected to the benefit Samsung prematurely reaped from his expertise, and subject to courts' interpretation and endless argument among lawyers. Why bother letting them argue about it? Just extend the penalty and you can skirt the entire valuation process. He agreed to two years, he serves two years, and then some, as reparations.
1|0@hardware^1440790489#cuiz41a:rdfox$:There shouldn't be any legal argument. The right to work is a human right and you can't contract away your right work anymore than you can contract away your right to live. It is tantamount to human slavery. This has been tested in courts all over the world and you have to go far down the list of backwards theocracies and dictatorships, past Mexico, past Iran, past Pakistan, maybe landing in North Korea before you'll find a court that won't throw out a morally reprehensible contract. (If you watched HBO's Silicon Valley, you'll remember this is a plot point.) I kind of feel bad for TSMC. They don't seem to realize that they've managed to set workers rights back 200 years in Taiwan and won't even be able to account for the damage this has done to their name, already in decline for a few years now.
0|0@hardware^1440800933#cuj5lyl:epsys$:oh, from that angle, yes. From the angle that he already contracted it out, no. 
1|0@hardware^1441053835#cum8plp:wwbulk$:He was compensated for not working and he agreed to it. Not sure tge reason for your rant.
1|0@hardware^1441061165#cumdbq6:rdfox$:The reason for my rant is it's a big blow for workers rights. In almost every country, if you pay someone to kill your wife, you can't then take them to court to compel them to do it. Non-compete clauses have the same legal status which is unenforceable because morally reprehensible. Now what's going to happen is that companies in Taiwan will add 2-year non-compete clauses to every employment contract. (If you've ever read an employment agreement you know that companies reserve every right they can legally reserve.) You will "agree to it" because it will be the same deal everywhere, and you will be "compensated" by every dollar they pay you. This will save a lot of money on turnover (it's estimated that turning over a white-collar position costs a company $100k-$300k) so is highly desirable from an employer's perspective. Not great from the worker's perspective as now they are basically slaves. Hopefully there will be strikes and protests until the policy is rectified. People don't like being enslaved.
