23|0@hardware^1440656811#cuh7gh1:Evidence_Of_Absence$:&gt; If you don't patent aomething, you don't get to claim industrial espionage Not true. I'm no expert on Korean or Taiwanese law, but in the U.S. the theft of a trade secret is a federal crime. &gt;he started working for samsung, had brains, helped Samsung, and now tsmc is suing because he decided to do something with his brain The latest and greatest semiconductor technologies aren't something you can cook up in your garage even if you're the smartest guy on the planet. Those ideas had to be based off of previous work, tested/vetted, etc. If you're using a company's time, money, prior art, etc. to move forward with ideas while working for them, they usually own that idea (unless they don't bother making you sign anything and that isn't the default state in whatever jurisdiction you're in, but I can assure you that any engineer working for a large multinational has to sign quite a few documents related to this).
