18|0@hardware^1440683049#cuhffa4:PVonMuter$:I work in intelligence and there's a lot of interesting things that happen among Asian governments and private corporations when it comes to corporate espionage. They have a completely different outlook on this than us. Japan will be the main example here, as they probably have the most robust corporate espionage system. The government actually spies for Japanese corporations and shares information with them. Not only is this viewed as a good thing in most Asian countries, it's encouraged. While Liang Mong-song went to Samsung of his own volition, it's highly likely Samsung was encouraged to take him by the ROK government. Additionally, Koreans are probably "offended" by the Taiwanese actions, because to them business is not as casual as Eastern business, it's war. I guess what I said doesn't really change the story, but I think it's important to learn how people think differently than us. It sorta changes the "heart" of the story. edit: Grammar/spelling
